Track 2: Eight Surdos, One Shadow
- Story & Context: For an outdoor rehearsal, Rafael invites additional percussionists to test how the groove behaves when the low drums are spread in a semicircle rather than stacked in one section. Late-afternoon sunlight throws all eight surdo shadows toward Hae-rin’s chair, creating a visual convergence that is not reflected in the rhythm: each drummer is playing a different function.

Min writes a spectral chord that begins as a clean Rhodes voicing but gradually separates into muted trumpet, bass clarinet, and synth partials. Hae-rin plays only three pitches across the entire track, but each one changes the harmonic interpretation of the drum mass.

Halfway through, the arrangement removes every upper percussion instrument. Eight low drums remain under one sustained ajaeng note, creating a massive but uncluttered center. The final minute reverses that image: the surdos disappear while caixa, agogô, and repinique continue under quiet bow noise.
